What there is to launch
=======================

`/bin` is a flat directory of ELF files with not one byte of metadata in it, so
a launcher that listed it could offer a filename and nothing else - no name
somebody chose, no picture, no arguments. What a program is called is written
down, and this is where.

One file, one program:

    app {
        name = Терминал
        icon = /etc/mayland/icons/terminal.qoi
        exec = /bin/mayterm
    }

Three keys and no others:

    name    what the launcher shows. Anything, in any language - the list is
            searched on what is written here
    icon    a QOI file. Missing, unreadable, or bigger than 64 by 64 and the
            row draws its tile without a picture rather than refusing to open
    exec    the command, with its arguments if it takes any. A path: nothing
            here searches a PATH, because nothing here has one

`title` is accepted for `name` and `command` for `exec`, because both are what
somebody writes first.

The order is the order the filenames sort in, which is why the shipped one is
numbered - the same convention `default.d` keeps. Drop in `50-editor.conf` and
it appears between 10 and 90 without touching anything else.

A key nobody knows about is complained about on the serial line with the file
and the line number it was on. A file that describes nothing usable - no name,
or no command - is skipped in silence: half a block somebody is still writing
is not an error yet.

Pictures
--------

Put a PNG or a JPEG in `assets/icons/` in the source tree and the build
converts it to QOI at 64 pixels a side, into `/etc/mayland/icons/`. Name it
here by the path it lands at. Anything already in QOI can be dropped straight
into `/etc/mayland/icons` on the running machine.

What draws this
---------------

Nothing in this directory says what the list *looks* like. That is
`/etc/mayland/shell/20-launcher.conf`, which describes the card, the search row
and the rows - and knows nothing about which programs are on it. The two halves
meet at one word: `source = apps.list`.
